        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy a <strong class="text-white">shop vac</strong> for debris, water, workshops, garages, cars and anything that would damage a household machine. Buy a <strong class="text-white">household vacuum</strong> for carpet, stairs, upholstery and everyday indoor dust, where a powered brush roll and sealed filtration do work that airflow alone cannot.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you only want one and your floors are hard surfaces, a shop vac with a <strong class="text-white">fine dust cartridge, a collection bag and a floor brush</strong> is a genuinely capable everyday vacuum. If you have carpet, it is not, and no amount of horsepower changes that.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And whichever you own, keep the rule that matters: <strong class="text-white">liquid goes only into a wet/dry vac with the paper filter removed</strong>. That one is about safety, not performance.
          </p>
